rs480: 3D-engine force-clock-then-read node for VAP/GA/TX/US/RB control registers

The display/overlay/panel/host-port/TV force-clock node forces one domain bit in
SCLK_CNTL (PLL 0x0d).  That cannot reach the 3D engine: the R300 vertex,
clip-barycentric and geometry-assembly force bits TCL (bit 13), CBA (bit 14) and
GA (bit 15) live in the separate R300_SCLK_CNTL2 register (PLL 0x1e), and the 3D
blocks feed each other (VAP -> GA -> SU -> RB), so forcing one block while its
upstream gates is unsafe.

This adds a 3D force-clock node that forces the WHOLE 3D clock set in both PLL
registers before the read -- VAP/TX/US/SU/RB in SCLK_CNTL and TCL/CBA/GA in
SCLK_CNTL2 -- reads one 3D control register, then restores both.  The force set
is grounded in radeon_reg.h and radeon_clocks.c r300_set_clock_gating.  The SCLK
status node also gains a SCLK_CNTL2 read so the GA/TCL/CBA gating state can be
observed (driver-mediated, no 3D MMIO) before any 3D read.

The table holds only 3D control and status registers (no instruction or
parameter memory); GA_IDLE and GB_Z_PEQ_CONFIG, already driver-read, anchor the
sweep, and the VAP entries are ordered last because VAP is the block whose write
wedged the reset-less K8.  Disarmed by default
(radeon_rs480_force_clock_3d_index == -1); arming and reading is an attended,
operator-gated, accepted-wedge step.

Assisted-by: Claude (Opus 4.8)
